expanded_sketch:
A strategy for proving the main theorem is outlined as follows.

\begin{itemize}
\item Reduce the Fourier--Jacobi models to the Bessel models via the (almost) equal rank theta correspondence, as in Wee Teck Gan and Benedict H. Gross and Dipendra Prasad, ``Symplectic local root numbers, central critical L values, and restriction problems in the representation theory of classical groups'' (2016) and Hiraku Atobe and Wee Teck Gan, ``Local theta correspondence of tempered representations and Langlands parameters'' (2018). This reduction requires two key ingredients: (i) "the irreducibility of the `big theta lifts' of tempered representations" and (ii) "the description of theta lifts in terms of L-parameters (the so-called Prasad's conjecture)."

\item Over $\BR$, since irreducibility of the big theta lifts in the (almost) equal rank case "is not known in general", instead "employ the stable range theta lift", for which irreducibility "has been established in \cite{MR3305312}."

\item Using stable range theta lift leads "naturally to certain non-tempered Gan--Gross--Prasad problems" (Bessel and Fourier--Jacobi). Relate these non-tempered cases to tempered ones "by the mean of non-tempered reductions", generalizing Colette M\oe glin, ``Conjecture locale de Gross-Prasad pour les groupes sp\'eciaux orthogonaux: le cas g\'en\'eral'' (2012), \S 1.4 to $\BR$.

\item Execute the proof in two parts: next prove the \emph{uniqueness} statement, namely that if a pair $(\pi,\widetilde\pi)$ in a Vogan L-packet admits a nonzero Fourier--Jacobi model, then it "must be the pair of representations determined by the distinguished characters defined in
\begin{equation}\label{distigulished character}
\begin{cases}
    \eta_{N_1}(a)= \epsilon\left(M^a\otimes N_1,\psi\right)\cdot \det\left(M^a\right)(-1)^{\frac{\dim N_1}{2}}\cdot \det\left(N_1\right)(-1)^{\frac{\dim M^a}{2}};\\[5pt]

    \eta_{M} (b) = \epsilon\left(M\otimes N_1^b,\psi\right)\cdot \det\left(M\right)(-1)^{\frac{\dim N_1^b}{2}}\cdot \det\left(N_1^b\right)(-1)^{\frac{\dim M}{2}},
\end{cases} 
\end{equation}
." Then prove \emph{existence}, i.e. "there does exist a pair $(\pi,\widetilde\pi)$ in each Vogan L-packet with a nonzero Fourier--Jacobi model." Combining these two parts "complete[s] the proof of the main theorem."
\end{itemize}

